# Drone Control System Using Dual ADSP-BF706 Controllers

## Project Overview

This project implements a custom drone control system using two ADSP-BF706 Blackfin+ processors configured as a transmitter and receiver.

The transmitter reads joystick and potentiometer inputs, processes the control values, and transmits them to the receiver through UART communication. The receiver generates four PWM control signals for interfacing with a KK2.1 flight controller.

## System Architecture

**Transmitter → UART Communication → Receiver → PWM Outputs → KK2.1 Flight Controller**

## Hardware Components

- 2 × ADSP-BF706 EZ-KIT Mini boards
- ADS1115 16-bit ADC module
- Joystick and potentiometer inputs
- KK2.1 flight controller
- SG90 servo motor (for testing)
- Supporting hardware and connecting wires

## Software and Development Tools

- CrossCore Embedded Studio (CCES) 2.12.1
- Embedded C
- UART communication
- I2C / TWI communication
- Timer-based PWM generation

## Transmitter Module

The transmitter processor reads joystick and potentiometer inputs. The ADS1115 module is used to acquire analog joystick values through the I2C interface.

Processed control data is transmitted to the receiver using UART0.

**UART connection:**
- PB8 – UART TX
- PB9 – UART RX

## Receiver Module

The receiver processor receives the transmitted control data and generates four PWM control channels.

**PWM channels:**
1. Throttle
2. Yaw
3. Pitch
4. Roll

These signals are interfaced with the KK2.1 flight controller.

## Key Features

- Dual ADSP-BF706 processor architecture
- Wireless-control architecture with a UART-based inter-board communication link
- Joystick input acquisition
- ADS1115-based analog input processing
- Four-channel PWM signal generation
- Flight controller interfacing
- Servo-based control testing

